                        "review": "So this is probably one of the weirdest and most unusual games I've come across in the last 40 years.\n\nYou're basically a sad little character who walks into a forest and sits down by a campfire. You know the feeling: night time, warmth from the fire, darkness all around, and someone to talk to. The whole game has a very deep and personal vibe. It constantly tries to convince you that it's your friend and that you can trust it with anything.\n\nSure, there is an option to disable data sharing, but honestly, I'd only truly trust that in a completely offline game with blocked network access. These days it's hard to trust anything that lives in the cloud. Tell it you don't like Trump and the next thing you know you're being deported back to Mexico. Obviously I'm joking, but you get the idea.\n\nAs a game, it's actually pretty good. I can absolutely see why some people would find it relaxing or even therapeutic. Sitting by the fire and having conversations creates a strangely comforting atmosphere.\n\nThat said, I'd be very careful about giving this game to children. It deals with personal topics and encourages players to open up emotionally. Adults can understand the difference between a game and real life, but younger players may not always make that distinction as easily.\n\nA very strange experience. Not a bad one, just one of the most unusual games I've played in a long time.\n\nI CAN RECOMMEND TO MY FRIEND EVEN MY GRANDMA.",
